        <div id="why-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Protein Matters More After 50: Menopause, Muscle and Bone
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most women in the US reach menopause around age 51, and the years either side of it bring changes that make protein more important than it was at 30. Muscle mass and strength decline gradually from midlife onward in both sexes, a process that becomes sarcopenia when it starts to affect strength and function. In women, research suggests the drop in estrogen around menopause is associated with an acceleration in that loss, along with a shift toward more fat, particularly around the abdomen. The evidence on exactly how much menopause itself adds, as opposed to aging and activity, is still developing, but the direction is consistent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bone follows a clearer pattern. Estrogen helps restrain the cells that break bone down, so when it falls, bone loss speeds up. The Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation estimates that women can lose up to about 20 percent of their bone density in the five to seven years after menopause, which is why osteoporosis is far more common in older women than in men. Muscle and bone are linked: stronger muscles pull on bones and load them, which helps maintain density, and better strength and balance lower the risk of the falls that turn weak bones into fractures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then there is anabolic resistance, the finding that older muscle responds less strongly to a given dose of protein than younger muscle does. Studies, many of them in older men, suggest that the muscle-building response to a meal needs more protein and more leucine, the amino acid that acts as the main trigger, to reach its peak. In practical terms, the 10 to 15 g of protein in a typical toast-and-coffee breakfast may do much less for a 55-year-old's muscle than it would for a 25-year-old's.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this calls for a special women's or menopause protein. It calls for enough protein, spread across meals, in servings large enough to count, alongside resistance training and adequate calcium and vitamin D. Our general <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powder for women</a> guide covers picks for every age; this page focuses on what changes in midlife. If you are well past 65 or managing frailty or appetite loss, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> guide addresses those needs directly.
          </p>

