        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: A Full Dose, Leucine and a Clean Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run lab tests or clinical trials for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, publicly listed certifications and published research on protein, aging muscle and bone, the method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are passing on what buyers commonly report.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first criterion was dose. Each product had to deliver roughly 25 to 30 g of protein in a normal serving, because that is the range where research on older adults suggests the muscle response approaches its peak. Many products marketed to women deliver 15 to 20 g per scoop, which is fine for a snack but falls short as a meal's worth of protein for midlife muscle. The second was protein quality. We favored whey and casein, which are complete and naturally rich in leucine, with whey isolate providing around 2.5 to 3 g of leucine in a 25 g serving. The plant pick uses a large, certified serving to narrow the gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third criterion was the label. We wanted short, fully disclosed ingredient lists, no proprietary blends, and none of the herbal menopause ingredients, such as black cohosh, red clover or dong quai, that some women-targeted powders add without meaningful evidence or with real interaction concerns. Where possible we chose products with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice status, which add independent screening for banned substances and, in NSF's case, label accuracy and contaminants such as heavy metals. Independent testing has found lead in many protein products, with plant-based ones averaging much higher than dairy, which is part of why dairy proteins dominate the list.
          </p>

          <p>
