        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein Women Over 50 Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            The US protein RDA for adult women is 46 g a day, or 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight, and it does not change with age. Many researchers consider that too low for older adults. The PROT-AGE study group, a panel of international experts, recommended 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram a day for healthy adults over 65, and at least 1.2 g per kilogram for those who are active, with higher amounts for people recovering from illness. The European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism has made similar recommendations. Those groups focused on people over 65, but the same logic increasingly applies to women in their 50s, when muscle and bone loss pick up.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a woman who weighs 68 kg (150 lb), 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram means roughly 70 to 80 g of protein a day, and around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram, often used for active adults trying to build or maintain muscle, means roughly 80 to 110 g. That is noticeably more than many women in midlife eat, particularly those who eat little meat, follow a plant-based diet or have cut back on food to manage weight. Survey data suggest that protein intake tends to fall with age, as appetite and meal sizes shrink.
          </p>

          <p>
            These are general ranges, not prescriptions. If you are carrying a lot of extra weight, basing the number on a healthy goal weight keeps it realistic. If you are losing weight, whether through diet or with a GLP-1 medication, protein needs relative to calories rise, because preserving muscle becomes harder; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1 users</a> guide covers that situation. And if you have kidney disease, the right intake may be lower, which is a decision for your doctor or a registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            To find your own starting point, our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> turns your weight and activity into a daily number, and our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> explains the reasoning by goal. Then look at a typical day honestly. If you are eating 50 or 60 g, adding one 25 g shake closes much of the gap without overhauling your diet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="per-meal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Per-Meal Problem: Why Breakfast Is the Easy Win
          </h2>

          <p>
            Daily totals are only half the story after 50. Because older muscle responds less to small doses, how protein is spread across the day seems to matter more than it does in younger adults. Research on older adults suggests that each meal needs roughly 0.4 g of protein per kilogram of body weight, or about 25 to 30 g for most women, to produce a strong muscle-building response, compared with less for younger people. Spread evenly, three meals at that level reach 75 to 90 g a day, which lines up neatly with the daily targets above.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trouble is that most people eat the other way around. A common pattern is 10 g at breakfast from toast, cereal or a pastry, 15 to 20 g at lunch from a salad or sandwich, and 40 g or more at dinner. The daily total may look fine, but two of the three meals fall below the level that older muscle responds to well, and a single large dinner cannot fully make up for it. The research on redistributing protein is still mixed, but given the evidence on per-meal dose, the change is low-risk and sensible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breakfast is where a powder helps most. Stir a scoop of unflavored whey into oatmeal after cooking, blend it into a smoothie with berries, milk and a spoonful of ground flax, or mix a flavored scoop into Greek yogurt. Any of those turns a 10 g breakfast into a 30 to 40 g breakfast in about a minute. Lunch is the next easiest fix: a shake alongside a lighter lunch, or a scoop stirred into soup once it is off the heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole foods can do the same job. Three eggs plus a cup of milk, a cup of cottage cheese, a generous serving of Greek yogurt, or a portion of salmon, chicken or tofu each gets close to the target, and they bring calcium, vitamin D, omega-3 fats or fiber that a powder lacks. The powder is simply a convenient way to hit the number at meals where food usually falls short. For thick, slow-digesting evening options, our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide goes further.
          </p>

