        <div id="lifting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Protein Works Best With Resistance Training
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein without training does relatively little for aging muscle. The strongest evidence for preserving and rebuilding muscle in midlife and beyond comes from resistance training, with protein amplifying the effect. Meta-analyses in older adults generally find that adding protein to a strength program produces modestly better gains in muscle mass than training alone, while protein supplements without training show little benefit. If you take one thing from this guide, make it this: the powder is the supporting act.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resistance training also matters directly for bone. In the LIFTMOR trial from Australia, postmenopausal women with low bone mass who did eight months of supervised, progressive high-intensity resistance and impact training improved bone density at the spine and hip compared with a low-intensity home program, with no increase in injuries under supervision. It was a small trial and the program was carefully coached, but it challenged the old assumption that women with low bone density should avoid heavy lifting. If you have osteoporosis, a history of fractures or joint problems, ask your doctor and start with a physical therapist or qualified coach who can progress you safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most women, the US Physical Activity Guidelines' recommendation is a sensible floor: muscle-strengthening activities that work all major muscle groups on at least two days a week, alongside aerobic activity and, for older adults, balance work. Squats, lunges or step-ups, a push such as a press or push-up, a pull such as a row, and a hip hinge such as a deadlift or bridge cover the basics. The weight should feel genuinely challenging by the last few repetitions, and it should increase gradually over weeks and months.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing your protein around workouts is a minor detail compared with getting enough at each meal, but it is an easy habit. A 25 to 30 g shake after a session, or a meal with that much protein within a couple of hours, makes sure the training stimulus has the building blocks it needs. Our explainer on <a href="/do-protein-supplements-build-muscle/">whether protein supplements build muscle</a> covers the research in more depth, and our <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">timing guide</a> explains why total intake matters more than the clock.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Calcium, Vitamin D and Bone Health
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein is one part of bone health; calcium and vitamin D are the others. The RDA for calcium rises from 1,000 mg a day to 1,200 mg a day for women at 51, and the vitamin D RDA is 600 IU (15 micrograms) a day up to age 70 and 800 IU (20 micrograms) after. Many women fall short on both. Dairy foods are the most concentrated common source of calcium, with a cup of milk or yogurt providing roughly 300 mg, and fortified plant milks, canned fish with bones, tofu set with calcium and some leafy greens add more.
          </p>

          <p>
            For years, a popular theory held that high-protein diets leach calcium from bone. Current evidence does not support that worry for people with adequate calcium intake. A 2017 systematic review and meta-analysis, supported by the National Osteoporosis Foundation, found no harm to bone from higher protein intakes and some suggestion of a small benefit at the spine. Protein is part of the structure of bone itself, and adequate protein is associated with better outcomes after hip fracture. In short, protein and calcium work together rather than against each other.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is one place where how you mix your powder makes a difference. A scoop of whey in a cup of milk adds roughly 300 mg of calcium and 8 g of extra protein to the shake. Some ready-to-drink shakes are fortified with calcium and vitamin D; the one on our list provides around half the daily value for calcium per bottle, per the label. That can help, but count it toward your totals, because taking calcium supplements on top of fortified foods can push intake past what you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whether you need calcium or vitamin D supplements is a question for your doctor, ideally alongside a bone density scan when appropriate. US guidelines recommend osteoporosis screening for women 65 and older and for younger postmenopausal women at increased risk. Food first is the usual advice for calcium, because supplements have been linked to kidney stones in some people, and the right vitamin D dose depends on your blood levels, sun exposure and medications.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="menopause-products" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Menopause Powders, Collagen and Soy: What the Evidence Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            Walk down a supplement aisle and you will find protein powders labeled for menopause, often with pink packaging, a smaller dose of protein and a blend of botanicals, adaptogens or added vitamins. The protein in them is usually the same whey, pea or collagen found in ordinary powders. The botanicals are the problem. Black cohosh has been linked to rare cases of liver injury, red clover and other plant estrogens have limited evidence for symptom relief, and many blends do not disclose doses. If you want help with hot flashes, sleep or mood, that is a conversation with your doctor about treatments with real evidence, including menopausal hormone therapy for women who are suitable candidates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen deserves a plain statement: it is an incomplete protein. It contains no tryptophan and very little leucine, so it is a poor choice as your main protein for building or maintaining muscle. Small studies, some funded by manufacturers, have reported changes in skin measures or bone markers with specific collagen peptides, but the evidence is preliminary. If you enjoy collagen in your coffee, there is no harm in it, but count it as a supplement to a complete protein, not a replacement. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powder</a> guide covers when it makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soy is more nuanced. Soy protein is complete and a reasonable plant option, and soy foods fit well in a balanced diet. Its isoflavones have been studied for hot flashes with mixed results, and The Menopause Society's 2023 review of nonhormone therapies did not recommend soy foods or isoflavone supplements as a treatment for hot flashes. For most women, a soy-based protein is fine as a protein, just not as a menopause remedy. If you have a history of hormone-sensitive cancer, ask your oncology team about soy and any supplement.
          </p>

          <p>
            The upshot is simple. The best protein powder for menopause is an ordinary, well-tested protein in a dose large enough to matter, paired with lifting, calcium and vitamin D. You do not need a menopause label, and you are often better off without one. Whey proteins remain the most practical choice; for an overview of how the main types compare, see our <a href="/types-of-protein-powder/">types of protein powder</a> explainer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="talk-to-your-doctor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Kidneys, Medications and When to Talk to Your Doctor
          </h2>

          <p>
            For healthy adults, protein intakes in the ranges discussed here have not been shown to harm the kidneys. Kidney function does decline gradually with age, though, and chronic kidney disease is common and often undiagnosed in older adults. If you have been told your kidney function is reduced, or you have diabetes or high blood pressure that has affected your kidneys, do not raise your protein intake without talking to your doctor or a registered dietitian, because the right target may be lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some medications interact with what is in a shake. Calcium, iron and soy can reduce the absorption of thyroid medication such as levothyroxine, so many pharmacists advise taking it several hours apart from calcium-rich shakes. Oral osteoporosis drugs such as alendronate typically must be taken on an empty stomach with plain water, well before any food or drink. Some powders include ingredients such as turmeric or added vitamin K that can matter if you take blood thinners. Your pharmacist can check any label against your medication list in a couple of minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other reasons to involve a professional include unexplained weight loss, a recent fracture or surgery, a history of eating disorders, liver disease, gout, food allergies, and any condition that limits fluid intake. If you are using a GLP-1 medication for weight loss, ask your prescriber about a protein target, because preserving muscle while losing weight is a known challenge. In each case, a registered dietitian can set a target that fits your health rather than a generic range.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you do talk to your doctor, take the actual product or a photo of the full label. Ask whether your protein target should change, whether you need a bone density scan, whether your calcium and vitamin D intake is adequate, and whether a resistance training program or referral to a physical therapist makes sense for you. Those four questions do more for midlife muscle and bone than any single product on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

