              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can protein powder help prevent osteoporosis?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. Adequate protein supports bone, and higher protein intakes do not harm bone when calcium intake is adequate, but osteoporosis prevention rests on resistance and impact exercise, enough calcium and vitamin D, avoiding smoking, limiting alcohol and, for some women, medication. Ask your doctor whether you need a bone density scan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is collagen good for women over 50?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Collagen is not a substitute for complete protein. It lacks tryptophan and has very little leucine, so it is a weak choice for maintaining muscle. Some small studies suggest effects on skin or bone markers, but the evidence is preliminary. Use it as an extra if you like it, and rely on whey, casein, soy or a dosed plant blend for muscle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time for women over 50 to take protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whenever it lifts a low-protein meal to about 25 to 30 g. For most women that is breakfast, where a scoop stirred into oatmeal, yogurt or a smoothie makes the biggest difference. After a strength workout is another good slot. Casein before bed is an option if you get hungry in the evening.
              </p>
            </div>

